Getting those credentials from Google can feel like a maze the first time you do it, but once you know exactly which buttons to click, it only takes a few minutes.
Here is the exact step-by-step process to generate your client-id and client-secret in the Google Cloud Console and plug them into your Spring Boot HRMS app.
Step 1: Create a Google Cloud Project
Go to the Google Cloud Console.
Log in with your Google account.
In the top-left corner, click the Project Dropdown (next to the Google Cloud logo) and click New Project.
Give your project a name (e.g., HRMS-Local-Dev) and click Create.
Once created, make sure that project is selected in the top dropdown menu.
Step 2: Configure the OAuth Consent Screen
Before Google gives you credentials, they want to know what the login screen should look like when employees try to log in.
On the left sidebar, click APIs & Services, then click OAuth consent screen.
Under "User Type", select External (this is easiest for local testing, even if you eventually want to lock it down to just your company's emails). Click Create.
App Information: * App name: Roima HRMS (This is what users will see when logging in).
User support email: Select your email address.
Developer contact email: Enter your email address again at the very bottom.
Leave the logo and app domains blank for now. Click Save and Continue.
Scopes: You don't need to add anything here. The default email and profile scopes are automatically included. Click Save and Continue.
Test Users: This is critical for local development! Because your app is in "Testing" mode, only specific emails can log in. Click Add Users and type in the exact Google email address you plan to use to test the login. Click Save and Continue.
Step 3: Create the Credentials (The ID and Secret)
Now that the consent screen is ready, we can generate the actual keys for your application.yml.
On the left sidebar, click Credentials.
At the top of the screen, click + Create Credentials, then select OAuth client ID.
Under Application type, select Web application.
Under Name, type something like Spring Boot Backend.
Step 4: Configure the URIs (The Most Important Step)
Google is extremely strict about security. If your Spring Boot app tries to redirect the user to a URL that Google doesn't explicitly recognize, Google will block the login with a redirect_uri_mismatch error.
Scroll down on that same page to the URIs section:
Authorized JavaScript origins:
Click + Add URI.
Enter: http://localhost:8080 (Your Spring Boot URL).
Enter: http://localhost:3000 (Your React URL, just to be safe).
Authorized redirect URIs: This must match your YAML file exactly. In your YAML, {baseUrl} translates to your server's host, and {registrationId} translates to google.
Click + Add URI.
Enter exactly this: http://localhost:8080/login/oauth2/code/google
(Note: Make sure there is no trailing slash at the end!)
Click Create at the bottom of the page.
Step 5: Copy to Your YAML File
A modal will pop up displaying "Your Client ID" and "Your Client Secret".
Copy the Client ID and paste it into your application.yml replacing YOUR_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID.
Copy the Client Secret and paste it into your application.yml replacing YOUR_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET.
(Pro-Tip: Never commit your Client Secret to GitHub! If you are pushing this code to a public repository, use environment variables like ${GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET} in your YAML file and pass the actual secret in via your IDE configurations).
Once you have these pasted in, your Spring Boot app is officially authorized to talk to Google.
package com.HRMS.HRMS.security;

import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.builders.HttpSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.configuration.EnableWebSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.config.http.SessionCreationPolicy;
import org.springframework.security.web.SecurityFilterChain;
import org.springframework.security.web.authentication.UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter;
import org.springframework.web.cors.CorsConfiguration;

import java.util.List;

@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class SecurityConfig {

    private final JwtAuthenticationFilter jwtAuthFilter; // Your existing JWT filter
    private final OAuth2LoginSuccessHandler oAuth2LoginSuccessHandler; // The bridge we built

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            // 1. Disable CSRF (Standard for JWT/Token-based APIs)
            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())
            
            // 2. Configure CORS so React (localhost:3000) can talk to Spring Boot
            .cors(cors -> cors.configurationSource(request -> {
                CorsConfiguration config = new CorsConfiguration();
                config.setAllowedOrigins(List.of("http://localhost:3000"));
                config.setAllowedMethods(List.of("GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE", "OPTIONS"));
                config.setAllowedHeaders(List.of("*"));
                config.setAllowCredentials(true);
                return config;
            }))

            // 3. Endpoint Permissions
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                // Allow public access to auth endpoints and OAuth2 redirects
                .requestMatchers("/api/auth/**", "/oauth2/**", "/login/**").permitAll()
                // Secure everything else (Requires either a JWT or an active login)
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )

            // 4. Configure OAuth2 Login for Google
            .oauth2Login(oauth2 -> oauth2
                // When Google login succeeds, trigger our custom handler to generate the JWT
                .successHandler(oAuth2LoginSuccessHandler)
            )

            // 5. Session Management
            // We keep it STATELESS because we want to rely on our JWTs, not server-side sessions
            .sessionManagement(session -> session
                .sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS)
            )

            // 6. Inject the JWT Filter
            // This tells Spring: "Before trying to authenticate a user with a username/password, 
            // check if they sent a valid JWT in the header!"
            .addFilterBefore(jwtAuthFilter, UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class);

        return http.build();
    }
}
